#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106903} :activity/id #uuid "70093123-9441-504b-9d19-1ff886bae1a9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049485 :topic/id #uuid "f8b72b93-d1e8-5602-88e3-d4c903a3465a" :topic/math-academy-id 1376} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106903 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107157 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given a point $P$ with position vector $\\mathbf{p},$ and another vector $\\mathbf{v}.$ \n\n![](images/b1/b1bb8d0cbe5dcbb24e224e8efc77e32db7a1657b245666aebd7b18107ee41149.png)\n\nThe **vector equation** of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ and passes through $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=p+tv,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is often called the **direction vector** of the line, while the vector $\\mathbf{r}$ denotes the position vector of an arbitrary point on the line.\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nthe vector equation of the line  that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ and passes through $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5bf1db5d-9d49-578a-b4a3-de51006d5ffd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7711 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "da72d9c9-d225-5f92-acf1-85ac68f4a4ea" :step/math-academy-id 44724 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106904}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106904 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105896 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105463 :question/id #uuid "9fa21896-8127-5ec5-aff0-7f811403db8c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12442"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcf425bc-d6ce-5cab-87ea-2d4d42270d09" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048780} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049715}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262614} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323733} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323735} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390434}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector Equation of a Line Given a Point and Direction Vector"} :step/id #uuid "cec448b9-8490-5eef-b44a-d69c60421dab" :step/math-academy-id 44725 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106905}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106905 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105897 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105464 :question/id #uuid "d1adb6fb-cd44-5a12-b892-6a6d2c99929d" :question/math-academy-id "e-12443"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6dee200-f517-5645-87ec-ccf36012e55e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262615} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262646} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431709} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492050}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector Equation of the Line Passing Through Two Given Points"} :step/id #uuid "a9e901bc-5996-547e-9050-8a27e0a73211" :step/math-academy-id 44728 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106906}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106906 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107158 :tutorial/content "There is a geometric intuition behind the formula $\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v},$ as shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/93/93b062a23c613184e7cdf725852401168b609a32056a5efcd24ceca2703d81d2.png)\n\nTo get to any point $A(x,y,z)$ on the desired line, starting from the origin $O,$ we can\n\n- first travel along the vector $\\mathbf{p}$ to get to the point $P$ on the line, and\n\n- then travel along the vector $t \\mathbf{v}$ to get to $A.$ (The scalar $t$ represents how far we travel forward or backward along the line in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}.$)" :tutorial/id #uuid "24f7081a-5ffa-545e-9268-8abff5267044" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7712 :tutorial/title "Geometric Intuition Behind the Formula"} :step/id #uuid "1882d34e-68eb-5861-b83e-9a6271a116a2" :step/math-academy-id 44731 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106907}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106907 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105898 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105465 :question/id #uuid "55ee3b10-087f-521e-ba37-12541e199408" :question/math-academy-id "e-12444"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c338d1eb-7e13-5089-8dd3-d5ca6eefd4ba" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051021}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262680} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431708} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492072}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points on a Line That Are a Fixed Distance From Another Point on the Line"} :step/id #uuid "7ec783db-e49b-5e4a-a359-af68f2d97438" :step/math-academy-id 44732 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106909}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106908 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105899 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105466 :question/id #uuid "69c3fc44-8b4d-521e-b207-0ea01d5921e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-12445"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f78fe749-e37e-53f2-879f-4c36931c1d6f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431705}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Segment"} :step/id #uuid "32a3a6c0-a3b3-531d-881f-278b4030a495" :step/math-academy-id 44735} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106909 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107159 :tutorial/content "![](images/44/443bbe1d201f1919fa2fb7ce3b632715cdc0e8dc77ff94bdc89f2cff7402b772.png)\n\nSuppose we wish to find a vector equation of the line *segment* whose endpoints are $P(3,-1,7)$ and $Q(3,4,4).$ \n\nTo do this, we need to find the vector equation of the corresponding line and then restrict the domain of the parameter $t.$\n\nFirst, we need to find a direction vector parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PQ}$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis equation describes every point on the line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PQ}.$ \n\nTo restrict this to points on the segment $\\overline{PQ}$ only, we set $t\\in [0,1].$ Thus, the vector equation of the line segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5t-1 \\\\ 7-3t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWriting the equation of a line segment in this way is sometimes called **parametrizing** the line segment since each point on the segment corresponds to a single parameter $t.$\n\n- Notice that $t=0$ gives us the position vector of $P{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5(0)-1 \\\\ 7-3(0)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Similarly, $t=1$ gives us the position vector of $Q{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5(1)-1 \\\\ 7-3(1)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSince increasing $t$ moves our vector $\\mathbf r (t)$ from $P$ to $Q,$ we say that the line segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is **traversed** *from* the point $P$ *to* the point $Q$ under this parametrization." :tutorial/id #uuid "5ec7269d-cea3-5a82-818b-cb048217f704" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7713 :tutorial/title "The Vector Equation of a Segment"} :step/id #uuid "50416523-59b6-572d-9328-3ca6a25a4c94" :step/math-academy-id 44736 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106908}}] :activity/title "The Vector Equation of a Line" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186106003}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431711} :activity/id #uuid "183eba74-280d-596d-84f9-614c47172a41" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049485 :topic/id #uuid "f8b72b93-d1e8-5602-88e3-d4c903a3465a" :topic/math-academy-id 1376} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431710 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431707 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4eacccd2-6f0b-56a0-af15-03a8df8a4aa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431680} {:answer/id #uuid "5df049c4-9c88-5398-9a72-6c30494cabef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431682} {:answer/id #uuid "80ce9f4b-db00-53a4-9818-0baaa275e656" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431683} {:answer/id #uuid "aed23c8f-009b-55ee-8619-d778834839e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431687} {:answer/id #uuid "e4ebdc55-f316-53b9-bb25-7a4ac5579ca8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431696}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431683} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b76e06b-b313-5d59-b449-7b9e393426a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431700}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5536a355-6822-5a47-a0a6-be384dfb79e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-126432" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the line that passes through the points $A(2,-3,5)$ and $B(3,1,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $A$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=a+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $a$ is the position vector of $A.$ \n\nWe can find a direction vector by computing the displacement vector between the given points $A(2,-3,5)$ and $B(3,1,-2)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{AB} \\\\  & =⟨3,1,-2⟩-⟨2,-3,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,4,-7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector equation of the line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "385f8efa-dead-57c8-bb0d-ca58862d7bb2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431713}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431711 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431709 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50a5c39e-5d72-5455-aec8-eb86a65caf2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431681} {:answer/id #uuid "b3d5149a-691b-58ab-8849-b5bdde35f7e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431688} {:answer/id #uuid "b4067977-bbce-543b-8574-d1b46a630254" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431689} {:answer/id #uuid "dd35f8ed-d92f-5862-8188-f38211a0649f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431695} {:answer/id #uuid "ebce683f-51aa-5b02-8297-81246cd6de01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431698}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431689} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcb53027-c7e0-55e6-85b6-4d8da4b5b3e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cf9c9d04-5ab5-558b-bf38-eae2eb3b3fea" :question/math-academy-id "q-35972" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the line that passes through the points $M(-1,-2,-3)$ and $N(2,3,4).$" :question/worked-solution "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $M$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=m+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $m$ is the position vector of $M.$ \n\nWe can find a direction vector by computing the displacement vector between the given points $M(-1,-2,-3)$ and $N(2,3,4)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{MN} \\\\  & =⟨2,3,4⟩-⟨-1,-2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,5,7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector equation of the line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "62732027-4291-5ae0-9766-6825a84c1896" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431712}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431712 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431708 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "416c72c0-8fb5-5d68-8b29-8af5bf68ead0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${Y}_{1}(14,7,1)$ and ${Y}_{2}(-1,-5,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431677} {:answer/id #uuid "4af680d4-f96a-54d6-a42c-84ed0f82803b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${Y}_{1}(7,12,2)$ and ${Y}_{2}(-10,-1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431679} {:answer/id #uuid "992d7033-f50d-5a79-9ab6-cbf67a0e1e27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${Y}_{1}(7,14,1)$ and ${Y}_{2}(-1,-10,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431685} {:answer/id #uuid "c5acab05-2c88-5065-9ff0-9e18e32d1ddd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${Y}_{1}(7,14,1)$ and ${Y}_{2}(-5,-1,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431690} {:answer/id #uuid "c6c7a32e-0763-553a-ae8f-36a7709abe9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${Y}_{1}(7,12,1)$ and ${Y}_{2}(-1,-5,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431691}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431685} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba4fda4c-a5fe-531d-a4cc-08d2ea5d8a53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431703}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c91c661d-e879-5f90-a5fb-ceb93bf19954" :question/math-academy-id "q-69807" :question/problem "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(3,2,1)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨1,\\,3,\\,0⟩.$ Find all the possible points $Y$ on that line such that $YP=\\sqrt{160}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨3,\\,2,\\,1⟩+t⟨1,\\,3,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $Y$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OY} & =⟨3,\\,2,\\,1⟩+t⟨1,\\,3,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PY} & =\\overset{\\to }{OY}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨3,\\,2,\\,1⟩+t⟨1,\\,3,\\,0⟩)-⟨3,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =t⟨1,\\,3,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,\\,3t,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PY=\\sqrt{160},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}YP & =\\sqrt{160} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PY}| & =\\sqrt{160} \\\\ |⟨t,\\,3t,\\,0⟩| & =\\sqrt{160} \\\\ \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+(3t)^{2}+{0}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{160} \\\\ \\sqrt{10{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{160} \\\\ \\pm \\sqrt{10}t & =4\\sqrt{10} \\\\ t & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 4$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{Y}_{1}}=⟨3,\\,2,\\,1⟩+4⟨1,\\,3,\\,0⟩=⟨7,14,1⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{Y}_{2}}=⟨3,\\,2,\\,1⟩-4⟨1,\\,3,\\,0⟩=⟨-1,-10,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${Y}_{1}(7,14,1)$ and ${Y}_{2}(-1,-10,1).$"} :step/id #uuid "6b10edff-9a52-5f78-b02b-67d728b26c18" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431710}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431713 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431706 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1339a533-dfd9-5aa8-8328-6269486f9c88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(1,3,2)$ and ${A}_{2}(1,4,-5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431675} {:answer/id #uuid "38ce3564-96d6-5aa9-8f31-aca072b73313" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(3,2,1)$ and ${A}_{2}(4,5,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431676} {:answer/id #uuid "9124bc93-158c-5515-bd32-81970e107298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(1,2,3)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,4,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431684} {:answer/id #uuid "cfbd0720-72fa-5325-aa30-34ea538948f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(1,2,3)$ and ${A}_{2}(1,3,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431693} {:answer/id #uuid "e55a4e80-0a0f-5330-8c1a-123763b280cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(1,-2,3)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,4,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431697}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431684} :answer-field/id #uuid "9028126a-fede-53d4-8337-146b30a620e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "194bde74-7427-5189-95d5-f403b6acd3c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-126965" :question/problem "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(3,3,1)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨-4,-2,4⟩.$ Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $AP=3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨3,3,1⟩+t⟨-4,-2,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OA} & =⟨3,3,1⟩+t⟨-4,-2,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PA} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨3,3,1⟩+t⟨-4,-2,4⟩)-⟨3,3,1⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨-4,-2,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4t,\\,-2t,\\,4t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PA=3,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AP & =3 \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PA}| & =3 \\\\ |⟨-4t,\\,-2t,\\,4t⟩| & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{(-4t)^{2}+(-2t)^{2}+(4t)^{2}} & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{16{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2}+16{t}^{2}} & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{36{t}^{2}} & =3 \\\\ \\pm 6t & =3 \\\\ t & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm \\frac{1}{2}$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{1}}=⟨3,3,1⟩+\\frac{1}{2}⟨-4,-2,4⟩=⟨1,2,3⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{2}}=⟨3,3,1⟩-\\frac{1}{2}⟨-4,-2,4⟩=⟨5,4,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${A}_{1}(1,2,3)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,4,-1).$"} :step/id #uuid "90b2f552-3c11-524e-897e-0564ec54b233" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431714}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431714 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431705 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ac29229-afea-5c3b-9d80-98e4c4817571" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431678} {:answer/id #uuid "9cebf039-4878-51b4-a3a9-d495dbdcdfb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431686} {:answer/id #uuid "c99bd56d-b16e-5836-93b7-59a7d5bea293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431692} {:answer/id #uuid "d760676b-c91b-55f6-95ef-1e2c1a8316a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431694} {:answer/id #uuid "fbd0ca81-588c-520c-9d6e-ae9aea95547e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431699}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431694} :answer-field/id #uuid "83f5ad19-8ad8-5ef4-8b4d-3a3b41390749" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431701}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "09c49afc-6085-50e6-863a-8dee0d62b478" :question/math-academy-id "q-162163" :question/problem "The equation below describes all the points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(-1,2,-3)$ and $Q(-2,0,4).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ a \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ b\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [c,1]\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand the vector equation of the  line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=2+7+0=9.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "983d1bf0-8aa7-5089-8bb5-ce39a8d426ba"}] :activity/title "The Vector Equation of a Line" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431674}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492105} :activity/id #uuid "cdd38161-b7a8-5f07-98fd-ab57d6d2c3c9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049485 :topic/id #uuid "f8b72b93-d1e8-5602-88e3-d4c903a3465a" :topic/math-academy-id 1376} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492082 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492050 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "076aefd7-f3ef-5e65-9a6e-fcef6787013e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491468} {:answer/id #uuid "31f26793-455b-5519-8bca-61ab48aea421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491554} {:answer/id #uuid "3e3dc15f-f6a0-5469-bd09-5e5682833602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491577} {:answer/id #uuid "45c99e89-541a-51a5-acd4-9fe79f6c950f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491586} {:answer/id #uuid "bafb0fb7-23f1-5dd9-8af3-0d84685adcd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491776}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491776} :answer-field/id #uuid "97d4e9ea-3206-52e6-90bd-f7265f05e6b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ba6720d7-134d-5884-b22f-7f4012ec3a77" :question/math-academy-id "q-126964" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the line that passes through the points $H(1,-2,3)$ and $S(4,-2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $H$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=h+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $h$ is the position vector of $H.$ \n\nWe can find a direction vector by computing the displacement vector between the given points $H(1,-2,3)$ and $S(4,-2,1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{HS} \\\\  & =⟨4,-2,1⟩-⟨1,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,0,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector equation of the line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1ec7ec1f-8794-50d0-a0c9-c532caebad39" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492093}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492089 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492072 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bce919e-a0d8-59f0-8778-d5b453d280a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${X}_{1}(2,-2,1)$ and ${X}_{2}(-4,-2,-5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491510} {:answer/id #uuid "44125a3b-2563-5069-8bbc-ae69a62e4b8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${X}_{1}(1,2,-1)$ and ${X}_{2}(-6,3,-9)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491584} {:answer/id #uuid "53ee61a5-d475-57b8-a0b9-a60723219d05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${X}_{1}(-2,2,1)$ and ${X}_{2}(-6,2,-7)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491614} {:answer/id #uuid "bf682358-c577-5e53-95cb-2e1a7d3008bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${X}_{1}(-2,0,1)$ and ${X}_{2}(1,2,-8)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491783} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f87627-6a47-5a19-b342-cf1d6b482b3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${X}_{1}(-2,4,-1)$ and ${X}_{2}(2,4,-6)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491820}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491614} :answer-field/id #uuid "5db99a8f-cee5-52ce-ae30-3cf87758843e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fae33bcb-2652-544e-838d-9fea7f4029a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-69799" :question/problem "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(-4,2,-3)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩.$ Find all the possible points $X$ on that line such that $XP=\\sqrt{20}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨-4,\\,2,\\,-3⟩+t⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $X$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OX} & =⟨-4,\\,2,\\,-3⟩+t⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PX} & =\\overset{\\to }{OX}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨-4,\\,2,\\,-3⟩+t⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩)-⟨-4,\\,2,\\,-3⟩ \\\\  & =t⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,\\,0,\\,2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PX=\\sqrt{20},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XP & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PX}| & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ |⟨t,\\,0,\\,2t⟩| & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(2t)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ \\sqrt{5{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ \\pm \\sqrt{5}t & =2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ t & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 2$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{X}_{1}}=⟨-4,\\,2,\\,-3⟩+2⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩=⟨-2,2,1⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{X}_{2}}=⟨-4,\\,2,\\,-3⟩-2⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩=⟨-6,2,-7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${X}_{1}(-2,2,1)$ and ${X}_{2}(-6,2,-7).$"} :step/id #uuid "3b25e9df-26b3-594e-b7e5-ee4df36a2c1f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492082}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492093 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106066 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12c0bd9a-8112-53b2-b018-8f7e3b0e9cac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491492} {:answer/id #uuid "6ddce68d-485d-5026-a8d0-15c7aadcd2ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491652} {:answer/id #uuid "88a898e9-97f9-5c0d-a2a9-e984a3785f23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491686} {:answer/id #uuid "8f4c0811-7dac-5fed-9809-20000ee340e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3-5{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491698} {:answer/id #uuid "9c7163c1-8542-5ca0-8417-6dfd8942bd73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{t}^{2}-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491725}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491686} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca2dac9f-c1e9-524d-9d24-eaf747039476" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "695c2a87-03bb-5036-ab74-93acc4cb34c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-162144" :question/problem "The equation below gives a parametrization of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(1,0,3)$ and $Q(1,2,0).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=i+a\\,j+b\\,k,\\,t\\in [c,1]\n$$\n\nWhat is $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(i+2\\,j)-(i+3\\,k) \\\\  & =2\\,j-3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=(i+3\\,k)+t(2\\,j-3\\,k)\n$$\n\nand the parametrization of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =(i+3\\,k)+t(2\\,j-3\\,k) \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j+(3-3t)\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,1].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=2t+(3-3t)+0=3-t.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4cb265da-0c2e-574b-9b97-7e10561660e5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492111}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492105 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106283 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ca1ce6f-1cfa-5862-abce-de665c4b16ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(-3,9,-4)$ and ${A}_{2}(-5,11,-8)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491545} {:answer/id #uuid "8d8daf29-4004-5012-a711-688c5f2c743f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(0,3,1)$ and ${A}_{2}(-4,7,-7)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491695} {:answer/id #uuid "9028b822-283b-56d0-899a-4c8406bf3508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(-3,9,-4)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,-11,8)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491699} {:answer/id #uuid "92e3c2fb-3736-54ce-91a9-4ddf0df4c5ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(4,-4,8)$ and ${A}_{2}(-4,4,-8)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491704} {:answer/id #uuid "aeea0b74-9973-5027-885f-71438bcfb05f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(0,3,1)$ and ${A}_{2}(4,-7,7)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491760}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491695} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6e72671-61e7-583b-875f-5e5b03441ae1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1704dfac-b6f1-5d12-b941-b68f37e7caca" :question/math-academy-id "q-32819" :question/problem "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(-2,5,-3)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨1,-1,2⟩.$ Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $AP=\\sqrt{24}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨-2,5,-3⟩+t⟨1,-1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OA} & =⟨-2,5,-3⟩+t⟨1,-1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PA} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨-2,5,-3⟩+t⟨1,-1,2⟩)-⟨-2,5,-3⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨1,-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,\\,-t,\\,2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PA=\\sqrt{24},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AP & =\\sqrt{24} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PA}| & =\\sqrt{24} \\\\ |⟨t,\\,-t,\\,2t⟩| & =\\sqrt{24} \\\\ \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+(-t)^{2}+(2t)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{24} \\\\ \\sqrt{6{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{24} \\\\ \\pm t\\sqrt{6} & =2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ t & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 2$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{1}}=⟨-2,5,-3⟩+2⟨1,-1,2⟩=⟨0,3,1⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{2}}=⟨-2,5,-3⟩-2⟨1,-1,2⟩=⟨-4,7,-7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${A}_{1}(0,3,1)$ and ${A}_{2}(-4,7,-7).$"} :step/id #uuid "bb116c13-04f6-5718-b5fc-2b6fa8103e9e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492089}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492111 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281392 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "358473a7-587c-552e-b379-4b8b0066f996" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279061} {:answer/id #uuid "51d5e23f-ed33-5e7e-a8a3-62ac07fa49f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279337} {:answer/id #uuid "a0eb9480-6bb7-57a6-881d-742802d0f9a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280021} {:answer/id #uuid "c3e4eccb-2a9d-50d7-a35d-b54bc39524f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280316} {:answer/id #uuid "c4cb9a51-b713-5d85-8c03-d80a8c668649" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280324}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279337} :answer-field/id #uuid "d49946f0-a578-5a3d-907d-fcbefbab0843" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3bcbe167-633d-5f59-83f8-d10ba4510f50" :question/math-academy-id "q-162168" :question/problem "The equation below describes all the points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(-6,2,-1)$ and $Q(-8,0,-1).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ b \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,c]\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand the vector equation of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=(-1)+(-2)+1=-2.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fa1f6cbe-7cdd-5cc9-a956-7908cb978446"}] :activity/title "The Vector Equation of a Line" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491447}]}
